Add an opt-in flat hash table to the CPU inducers.

CPUInducer and CPUHeteroInducer map global ids to local ids with a node-based
std::unordered_map and do about three hash operations per edge: one insert, then two
operator[] lookups, one of them repeated for every neighbor of the same source. The flat
implementation uses an open-addressing table (power-of-two capacity, linear probing, growth by
doubling) with one table operation per edge and one per source, and produces output identical
to the std path, including local id order. Its memory is bounded by the distinct ids of a
batch: the max_num_nodes constructor hint only pre-sizes the table up to a fixed cap and the
table grows past it on demand.

Both implementations stay compiled. GIGL_GLT_INDUCER_IMPL=std (default) keeps the original code
path; =flat selects the new one; any other value falls back to std with a one-time warning on
stderr. The variable is read once per process, when the first inducer is constructed.
pywrap.inducer_impl() returns the selected name.

